-- title:
--   Every literal word has an exact zero-board lift
-- statement:
--   Write $I_n=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$, with cyclic arithmetic modulo $n$ and integer representatives for inequalities; $[x]_n$ denotes the least nonnegative residue when $n>0$. For an integer board $d:I_n\to\mathbb Z$, put $F_i=i+d_i$, $\lambda_i=F_i\bmod n$, $C_{ij}=-\lfloor(((j-i)\bmod n)+d_j-d_i)/n\rfloor$, $B_i=\sum_j\max(-C_{ij},0)$, and $I(d)=\sum_{i<j}|\lfloor(F_j-F_i)/n\rfloor|$. A balanced lift of a permutation $p$ with phase $c$ means $\sum_i d_i=0$ and $i+d_i\equiv p^{-1}(i)+c\pmod n$. Words use a forward cyclic rotation $L$ and the exchange $X=(0\ 1)$; $\operatorname{ev}(w)$ is their permutation product and $|w|$ their number of letters. Let $n\ge2$ and let $w$ be any word. Then
--
--   $$
--   \exists d:\quad d\text{ lifts }\operatorname{ev}(w)\text{ at phase }\#_L(w),\quad\operatorname{run}(w,d)=0.
--   $$
--
--   The construction couples the affine board and phase to the same actual word.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/snow-ghost/glushkov-lx-lean/blob/8cddcd222b591673dc63475d9d45b9ed5c6eb92c/LX/GreedyWordNormalForm.lean#L12-L29

